Scientific_Context:
The paper proposes a methodology using genetic tools in mice to identify and characterize vagal PIEZO2 neurons as blood volume receptors. This involves selective genetic knockout of Piezo2 (Vagal-ΔPiezo2, Vagal-ΔPiezo1/2) and/or ablation of PIEZO2 neurons (PIEZO2-vagalABLATE) to assess their role in cardiovascular stability. The approach integrates several techniques: an adapted tilt-table test for mice to assess posture-dependent blood pressure changes, whole-nerve electrophysiological recordings of the vagus nerve trunk to measure heartbeat-coupled responses and their modulation by blood volume changes, optogenetic stimulation of PIEZO2 neurons, and in vivo two-photon calcium imaging of vagal ganglia to identify responsive neurons to airway stretch and blood volume infusion. Anatomical mapping using AAV-flex-tdTomato reporters in Cre-driver mice (Vglut2-ires-cre, Piezo2-ires-cre, Npy2r-ires-cre) is also used to visualize neuronal projections and terminal types in the heart. The study also employs a tail-bleeding-induced haemorrhage model to evaluate physiological compensation for blood loss.

[Datasets used]: previously published vagal/glossopharyngeal cell atlas: A single-cell RNA-sequencing study used for UMAP plots depicting expression of Piezo2 and Npy2r across vagal/glossopharyngeal sensory neuron types.

Electrophysiological recordings on the tilt table were not technically achievable;

The paper proposes an expanded Registry of candidate cis-Regulatory Elements (cCREs) for human and mouse genomes. This expansion leverages new datasets from ENCODE Phase IV (ENCODE4) and enhanced computational methods. The methodology involves anchoring cCREs on representative DNase hypersensitivity sites (rDHSs) and representative transcription factor clusters (rClusters), then annotating their functions using H3K4me3, H3K27ac, and CTCF ChIP-seq data, as well as incorporating thousands of transcription factor ChIP-seq and ATAC-seq datasets. The cCREs are classified into eight categories based on distance to annotated transcription start sites (TSS) and combinations of biochemical signals, including three new categories: CA-TF cCREs, CA cCREs, and TF cCREs. The method also includes functional characterization using assays like STARR-seq, MPRA, CRISPR perturbation, and transgenic mouse assays, and introduces a novel method called CAPRA (CRE-centric Analysis and Prediction of Reporter Assays) to calculate cCRE-specific STARR scores and resolve enhancer and silencer activities.

[Datasets used]: Registry of 0.9 million human and 300 thousand mouse candidate cis-Regulatory Elements (cCREs): The previously developed Registry of cCREs by The Encyclopedia of DNA Elements (ENCODE) consortium, which mapped biochemical signals across hundreds of cell types and tissues. | expanded Registry: The main output of this study, including 2.37 million human and 967 thousand mouse cCREs, leveraging new ENCODE datasets and enhanced computational methods. | ENCODE Phase III (ENCODE3) Registry of candidate cis-Regulatory Elements (cCREs): The previous version of the ENCODE Registry, which the current work significantly expanded upon. | ENCODE Phase IV (ENCODE4) data: New datasets produced during ENCODE Phase IV, used to expand the Registry and improve computational methods. | STARR-seq: A genome-wide functional assay used to test the activities of millions of genomic regions, providing quantitative enhancer and silencer activity maps. | massively parallel reporter assays (MPRA): A functional assay used to characterize transcriptional regulatory elements. | CRISPR perturbation assays: Functional assays, including CRISPRi-FlowFISH, used to characterize the activity of genomic regions in their native chromatin context. | transgenic mouse enhancer assays: Functional assays performed in vivo to validate enhancer activity in specific tissues. | DNase hypersensitivity sites (rDHSs): Representative sites used as anchors for defining cCREs, indicating open chromatin regions. | H3K4me3, H3K27ac, and CTCF ChIP-seq data: Chromatin immunoprecipitation sequencing data used to annotate the functions of cCREs based on specific histone modifications and transcription factor binding. | transcription factor ChIP-seq and ATAC-seq datasets: Additional datasets incorporated to enable annotation of elements in low-accessibility regions and biosamples lacking DNase-seq. | VISTA Enhancer Database: A database of tissue-specific human enhancers, used to supplement previously tested regions for transgenic mouse enhancer assays. | K562 STARR-seq experiment ENCSR661FOW: A specific STARR-seq experiment in K562 cells used to identify stringent and robust STARR silencer cCREs. | ChromHMM annotations: Chromatin state segmentations from the ENCODE Project, used to evaluate the chromatin state context of silencers. | GENCODE annotated protein coding gene: A database of gene annotations used to identify the closest gene to each cCRE and extract gene quantifications. | gene quantifications in K562 (ENCFF421TJX): Transcripts per million (TPM) quantifications for genes in K562 cells, available on the ENCODE portal. | MAFF and MAFK ChIP-seq data: ChIP-seq data for transcription factors MAFF and MAFK in K562 and HepG2 cells, used to define MAFF/MAFK+ cCREs. | RNAPII ChIA-PET, CTCF ChIA-PET and Hi-C: 3D chromatin interaction data from K562 cells, used to link candidate cCREs to genes via chromatin loops. | GWAS-reported variants: Genetic variants associated with traits, used in the VESPA pipeline to identify candidate GWAS genes. | Zoonomia mammalian genomes: A collection of mammalian genome alignments (240-way mammalian alignment) used to assess evolutionary conservation of cCREs. | RepeatMasker elements: A database of repetitive elements used to analyze the overlap of cCREs with different repeat classes.

This overall rate likely underestimates the true fraction of functional cCREs, as most assays were performed in a limited number of cell types and are biased toward detecting enhancer activity;
